"if (ishuman(ptr)) adjalign(-sgn(u.ualign.type));"

means you will lose one point of alignment per species genocided.  The
Minetown Watch consists of exactly two species, so eliminating them by
genocide costs you two points of alignment.

Killing a goblin (as a lawful character) will gain you three points of
alignment. (Seriously!  Check out xkilled() in mon.c, set_malign() in
makemon.c, and the data in monst.c to see why.)

Unless you are a pacifist, your lawful character will not suffer a
*meaningful* alignment penalty as a result of eliminating the watch
by means of genocidal magic.

>While not normally a problem, this may be not that good
>an idea when you're a. low-level enough that genociding the Watch makes
>a difference and b. about to sacrifice in, and possibly try to convert,
>the Minetown temple.

If you are low enough level that genociding the watch materially
increases your chances of surviving committing a crime in Minetown,
attempting to convert the minetown altar is suicidally stupid.

Aligned priests have more AD_PHYS damage output (4d10 + weapon damage)
than anything you'll meet that shallow except a mumak, and they can also
blind you for 200 turns, summon a plague of insects, paralyse you for
15 turns, confuse you for 11 turns, curse your equipment, blast you
with lightning for 8d6 damage (if they haven't had their level nerfed
through being too shallow), or cause wounds to open on your person for
6d6 damage (5d6 if they've been nerfed).
